This project runs the Laya decision model in the browser.

Vendored source (Apache-2.0):
- Laya, https://github.com/NandhaKishorM/laya
  Copyright Convai Innovations and Laya contributors.
  Files adapted into this repository under src/vendor/laya-ts/:
    - agent.ts, common.ts, providers.ts, tokenizer.ts, hooks.ts, structured.ts
    - scripts/export_laya_ts.py (from laya-ts/scripts/export_onnx.py)
  Modifications:
    - providers.ts is browser-only, bundles ONNX Runtime statically, uses a flat
      Float32Array encoder→head handoff, and disables graph optimization on the
      WebGPU encoder path to avoid an unsupported fused LayerNorm;
    - providers.ts adds a WebGPU capability preflight for the fp16 encoder
      (adapter + `shader-f16`) and exports the out-of-memory / WebGPU limit /
      device-lost / model-output classifier the inference worker uses to shrink
      its batch and to decide on the CPU fallback;
    - providers.ts adds `ProviderPlan` (WebGPU-first, WASM-second), the pure
      `decideWasmFallback` / `nextBackend` policy the worker uses to rebuild on
      WASM and restart an analysis, `SessionProvider.dispose`, and
      `assetIsCached` so a rebuild reuses Cache Storage instead of the network;
    - agent.ts adds `predictBatch` (multi-state batching) and decodes through a
      shared `_answerFor` helper;
    - agent.ts adds `dispose()` and releases the provider's sessions when
      `Agent.load` fails after they were created;
    - agent.ts validates every head pass (`validateHeadOutput`) and every decoded
      answer (`validateAnswer`) for row counts, expected lengths and finiteness,
      raising a Japanese error instead of returning NaN probabilities;
    - the exporter writes an fp16 encoder with an fp32 output and an fp32 head,
      and emits a parity fixture.

